0,"Tektitek language - WikipediaPresentation on Tektiteko by linguist Juventino de Jesus ...20. Tektiteko. Orgullosamente, hablo mi idioma maya. - YouTubeMaya music - WikipediaMayan Languages.net - TektitekTectitec language. Evangelism tools, church planting ...",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tektitek_language,"Teko is spoken in the municipalities of Amatenango de la Frontera, Frontera Comalapa and Mazapa de Madero in the state of Chiapas, Mexico. [6] In the Huehuetango Department it is spoken in the municipalities of Tectitán and Cuilco. May 5, 2010 · Linguist : Juventino de Jesus Pérez AlonzoImage and sound : José ReynèsEditing : Caroline LaurentThe linguist presents Tektiteko: one of the Mayan languages ... Aug 14, 2025 · 20. Tektiteko. Orgullosamente, hablo mi idioma maya. Orgullosamente, “hablo mi idioma maya” ¡Habla! Porque tienes el derecho. Indigenous Mayan music can still be heard today in Yucatán and Chiapas. The tunkul (a slit drum) and the bulalek (water drum) are played in Yucatán during Christian religious festivities. Tektitek, like many indigenous languages, possesses distinct phonetic, morphological, and syntactic traits. Its vowel and consonant system, while intricate, is beautifully balanced, resulting in a melodic rhythm when spoken. One of the remarkable features of Tektitek is its use of evidentials. Short audio Bible stories and evangelistic messages that explain salvation and give basic Christian teaching. Each program is a customised and culturally relevant selection of scripts, and may include songs and music.",Tektiteko,Mayan
1,Maya music - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maya_music,Indigenous Mayan music can still be heard today in Yucatán and Chiapas. The tunkul (a slit drum) and the bulalek (water drum) are played in Yucatán during Christian religious festivities.,Tektiteko,Mayan
2,Tektiteko Language Translators: People of Guatemala & Mexico,https://languagexs.com/tektiteko-mayan-language-guatemala-mexico/,"Tektiteko is a fascinating Mayan language spoken by a small group of people in Guatemala and Mexico. This article examines the origins, characteristics, and current status of Tektiteko, also known as Tectiteco or Teco.",Tektiteko,Mayan
3,"Presentation on Tektiteko by linguist Juventino de Jesus ...20. Tektiteko. Orgullosamente, hablo mi idioma maya. - YouTubeMaya music - WikipediaMayan Languages.net - TektitekTectitec language. Evangelism tools, church planting ...",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dRtkJWln2iM,"May 5, 2010 · Linguist : Juventino de Jesus Pérez AlonzoImage and sound : José ReynèsEditing : Caroline LaurentThe linguist presents Tektiteko: one of the Mayan languages ... Aug 14, 2025 · 20. Tektiteko. Orgullosamente, hablo mi idioma maya. Orgullosamente, “hablo mi idioma maya” ¡Habla! Porque tienes el derecho. Indigenous Mayan music can still be heard today in Yucatán and Chiapas. The tunkul (a slit drum) and the bulalek (water drum) are played in Yucatán during Christian religious festivities. Tektitek, like many indigenous languages, possesses distinct phonetic, morphological, and syntactic traits. Its vowel and consonant system, while intricate, is beautifully balanced, resulting in a melodic rhythm when spoken. One of the remarkable features of Tektitek is its use of evidentials. Short audio Bible stories and evangelistic messages that explain salvation and give basic Christian teaching. Each program is a customised and culturally relevant selection of scripts, and may include songs and music.",Tektiteko,Mayan
